      "latin": "Computēmus.",
      "status": "recommended",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "Let’s reckon up: the bill, please.",
      "evidence": [
        "CIL IX 2689: <span lang=\"la\">Cōpō, computēmus.</span>",
        "Stat. Silv. 4.9.6: <span lang=\"la\">licet, ecce, computēmus!</span>"
      ],
      "comment": "What the traveller says to the innkeeper on the tombstone relief from Aesernia, “Innkeeper, let’s reckon up”, before the two go through the wine, the bread, and the rest. <span lang=\"la\">Computāre</span> is “to reckon up” (<span lang=\"la\">digitīs ratiōnem computat</span>, Plaut. Mil. 204). The bill itself is <span lang=\"la\">ratiō</span> (see “bill”): <span lang=\"la\">Ratiōnem, quaesō.</span>"
    }
  ],
